pin_control — pyvcp replacement for the removed `sim_pin` utility

sim_pin was a Tk panel that attached directly to a pin named on its command
line, e.g.

    [APPLICATIONS]
    APP = sim_pin  mv.power-on  mv.apply-offsets/mode=pulse

It went with the Tcl HAL binding (tcl/hal.so). A pyvcp panel does the same job
with one difference: it owns its own pins (pyvcp.<halpin>) and you connect them
to the target in a HAL file, rather than naming the target on a command line.

Files:
  pin_control.xml       the panel — one widget per sim_pin mode
  pin_control_load.hal  `load pyvcp <pyvcp> xml=pin_control.xml`, a HALFILE so
                        the pins exist before the GUI starts
  pin_control.hal       POSTGUI_HALFILE — connects the panel pins to signals
  pin_control.ini       [TASK]PYVCP_INSTANCE names the instance to milltask so
                        /info reports the panel to AXIS

Widget equivalents:
  sim_pin /mode=toggle, /mode=hold  ->  checkbutton   (latched)
  sim_pin /mode=pulse               ->  button        (true only while held)
  sim_pin numeric Entry + Set       ->  spinbox       (scale for a bounded range)
  (sim_pin had no read-back; the led/number here show what reached the signal)

To drive real pins, point the right-hand side of each net in pin_control.hal at
the pin you want. The panel needs no editing unless you want different widgets.
